{
 "id": "l06",
 "label": "6",
 "title": "Personal and demonstrative pronouns",
 "summary": "I, we, you, he, she, they, and this and that",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Use the personal pronouns akó, kamí, táyo, ikáw/ka, kayó, siyá and silá",
  "Know the difference between kamí and táyo",
  "Shorten ay to 'y after a word ending in a vowel",
  "Use itó, iyán and iyón, and their plural forms",
  "Use this and that before and after a noun"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What a pronoun does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **pronoun** replaces a noun so a sentence is less repetitive. Two important types are used often:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Type",
      "Words"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Personal pronouns",
       "[[akó|pron]], [[kamí|pron]], [[táyo|pron]], [[ikáw|pron]], [[kayó|pron]], [[siyá|pron]], [[silá|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "Demonstrative pronouns",
       "[[itó|pron]], [[iyán|pron]], [[iyón|pron]]"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The personal pronouns in this lesson are **nominative** in form. They can only be used as the **subject** of a sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Pronoun",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[akó|pron]]",
       "I"
      ],
      [
       "[[kamí|pron]]",
       "we (he/she and I, not the person spoken to)"
      ],
      [
       "[[táyo|pron]]",
       "we (you, he/she and I; or you and I)"
      ],
      [
       "[[ikáw|pron]] / [[ka|pron]]",
       "you (one person)"
      ],
      [
       "[[kayó|pron]]",
       "you (plural, excluding the speaker; also polite)"
      ],
      [
       "[[siyá|pron]]",
       "he or she"
      ],
      [
       "[[silá|pron]]",
       "they"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Personal pronouns"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Akó: I",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Akó** means *I*. As a subject it can come at the **beginning** of the sentence, or, in the inverted order, **after the predicate**."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Akó ay si John.",
       "en": "I am John.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Akó",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "am",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "article (one name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Akó ay laláki.",
       "en": "I am a man.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Akó",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "am",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "laláki",
         "man",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masípag akó.",
       "en": "Industrious I am. (I am industrious.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masípag",
         "industrious",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "akó",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Malusóg akó.",
       "en": "Healthy I am. (I am healthy.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Malusóg",
         "healthy",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "akó",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Ay becomes 'y"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In the subject-predicate order, **ay** may be shortened to **'y** when the word before it **ends in a vowel**. This is also used after **kamí**, **táyo** and other pronouns ending in vowels."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ako'y si John.",
       "en": "I am John.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ako'y",
         "I am",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "article (one name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ako'y laláki.",
       "en": "I am a man.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ako'y",
         "I am",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "laláki",
         "man",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ako'y nag-áaral.",
       "en": "I am studying.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ako'y",
         "I am",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "nag-áaral",
         "studying",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Kamí and táyo: two kinds of we",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English has one *we*. Tagalog has two:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Means",
      "Includes the person you are talking to?"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[kamí|pron]]",
       "we in the sense of he/she and I",
       "No"
      ],
      [
       "[[táyo|pron]]",
       "we in the sense of you, he/she and I, or you and I",
       "Yes"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Kamí or táyo?"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kamí ay sina John at Helen.",
       "en": "We are John and Helen.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kamí",
         "we (not you)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "sina",
         "article (2+ names)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Helen",
         "Helen",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Kamí'y magkapatid.",
       "en": "We are brothers and sisters.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kamí'y",
         "we (not you) are",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "magkapatid",
         "siblings",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mababaít kamí.",
       "en": "Good we are. (We are good.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mababaít",
         "good (plural)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kamí",
         "we (not you)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mahihírap kamí.",
       "en": "Poor we are. (We are poor.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mahihírap",
         "poor (plural)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kamí",
         "we (not you)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Táyo ay sina John at Mary.",
       "en": "We are John and Mary.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Táyo",
         "we (with you)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "sina",
         "article (2+ names)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Mary",
         "Mary",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Táyo'y magkaibígan.",
       "en": "We are friends.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Táyo'y",
         "we (with you) are",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "magkaibígan",
         "friends",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mababaít táyo.",
       "en": "Good we are. (We are good.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mababaít",
         "good (plural)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "táyo",
         "we (with you)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masisípag táyo.",
       "en": "Industrious we are. (We are industrious.)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masisípag",
         "industrious (plural)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "táyo",
         "we (with you)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Pronoun position",
     "t": "Pronouns may come at the **beginning** of the sentence before the verb, or at the **end** after the predicate, depending on the word order."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Ikáw, ka and kayó: you",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ikáw** expresses *you* at the **beginning** of a sentence. In the **inverted** order, where the subject comes after the predicate, it is shortened to **ka**."
