  {
   "title": "Itó, iyán and iyón: this and that",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English *this* and *that* have three Tagalog equivalents. Which one you use depends on **where the thing is**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Said",
      "Means",
      "The thing is…"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[itó|pron]]",
       "i-TO",
       "this",
       "very near or close to the person speaking"
      ],
      [
       "[[iyán|pron]]",
       "i-YAN",
       "that",
       "near the person spoken to"
      ],
      [
       "[[iyón|pron]]",
       "i-YON",
       "that, over there",
       "far from both the speaker and the person spoken to"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Three demonstratives"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Itó is followed by **ay** in the subject-predicate order, and ay may be shortened to **'y** after itó. In the inverted order, such as **Maliít itó**, ay is dropped."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Itó ay si John.",
       "en": "This is John.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Itó",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "article (one name)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Itó'y batà.",
